    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis presents a new approach to stochastic simulation of fracture networks. The simulation procedure mimics fracture propagation in order to generate geologically acceptable fracture patterns. Fractures are grown following a-priori rules of propagation inferred for each specific host geological environment.The algorithm developed is a hybrid pixel / object approach where fractures are constructed as objects which are themselves sets of connected pixels rather than parametric objects. Working on a pixel grid gives extreme flexibility for modeling and the fractures are not required to take any pre-specified shape. In addition, an incremental growth of the fractures, pixel by pixel, allows generating curvilinear or undulating fractures. It also allows the fractures to be stopped in their propagation, e.g. when they encounter pre-simulated open fractures or shale laminae.The fracture simulation model requires minimal and experimentally available data input, essentially about the spatial distribution of fracture densities. Fracture density is the one information which can be derived from all sources of data. This thesis also investigates how variations in fracture density distributions affect the simulation of discrete fractures and how this, in turn, impacts fluid flow through the fracture system.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
